 In Rust, you can use match statements instead of multiple if-else conditions to achieve the same result with more concise and efficient code. Here's how to implement the `is_even` function in Rust:

```rust
fn is_even(n: i32) -> bool {
 match n {
     0 => true,
     1 | 3 | 5 | 7 | 9 | _ if n.abs() % 2 != 0 => false,
     _ => {
         // If we reach this point, it means that n is even
         true
     },
 }
}
```

