 I'd be happy to help you write C code for converting `argv` (an array of strings passed as arguments to the main function in C) into a single `char*` or `cstring`. Here's a simple example that concatenates all elements of `argv` into a single string, using dynamic memory allocation:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    size_t total_length = 0; // Initialize total length to zero
    size_t current_length = 0; // Initialize current length to zero
    size_t i;                // Loop index

    // Calculate the total length needed for the concatenated string
    for (i = 0; i < argc; ++i) {
        total_length += strlen(argv[i]) + 1; // Add length of each string and one space between them
    }

    // Allocate memory for the new string with the calculated length
    char *new_str = malloc(total_length * sizeof(char));
    if (!new_str) { // Check if memory allocation was successful
        fprintf(stderr, "Memory allocation failed\n");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    // Concatenate all strings from argv into new_str
    strcpy(new_str, ""); // Initialize new_str to an empty string
    for (i = 0; i < argc; ++i) {
        strcat(new_str, argv[i]); // Concatenate next argument to new_str
        strcat(new_str, " ");     // Add a space between arguments
    }

    printf("Combined string: %s\n", new_str); // Print the combined string

    free(new_str); // Don't forget to free the allocated memory!
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

This code calculates the total length required for the concatenated string, allocates memory for it, copies the strings from `argv` into the new string, and prints the result. Note that this is just a simple example, and more robust error checking and handling might be needed

